Z||000015||2xsess||02-21-2001||07:36 AM||||hmmmm...that would explain the loud screaming noises I've been hearing, Hoop, can ya keep it down a bit, I can hear you cussing all the way down here  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Seriously though, sorry to hear how much trouble you're having with this. <P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>AOL IM : Only2xsess<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2xsess.gif">      <BR> <A HREF="http://kresgeus.homestead.com/FieroPage.html" TARGET=_blank>Visit My Website</A>||||reg||1
Z||000016||bHooper||02-24-2001||01:13 PM||||ok, i'm really about to get angry now!<P>first off i would like to thank 007Doug for the rear manifold, that he sent me.  thanks!  it is in excellent shape.<P>secondly, having received said manifold, i decided it was time to replace the previous one that has a huge crack on the weld at one of the flange's (later, once i had pulled the manifold, the flange litterally fell off onto the floor!).  anyway, so i start removing bolts (that had been soaking in PB blaster since last night), when i notice that the one  from just above the altenator, is very short....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">  <P>well i finish pulling all the other bolts, and the flange/footer falls on the floor.  at this point i grab the mirror and decide to take a look... well, it appears that i was not the culprit that broke the bolt... it has been broken for some considerable time!<P>which brings me to my question <B>what the heck do i do now!?</B>  since i am using the liquid gasket material, that Ed Parks recomended, can i get by with reinstalling whats left of the small bolt, to get me by for a bit?<P>i really want to put a 3.4 liter in the car next winter, and i don't want to spend much money on this engine.  <I>well, i just went and looked at the bolt again, so much for reinstalling it.  there is no threads available!</I><P>so, can i use the permatex gasket stuff, 5 studs and flang nuts, and some sort of "clamp" on this one!?<P>thanks!<BR>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000017||rogergarrison||02-24-2001||02:25 PM||||thats why I used Felpro manifold gaskets, 3rd year, still no leaks (knock on wood) For some reason, Ive never trusted any liquid gasket material. Your prob confirms my beliefs. I also used stainless steel studs, hex flange nuts.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Mark||02-24-2001||02:34 PM||||Hoop, don't panic....not just yet!<P>I also had a broken manifold bolt on my rear manifold.  And yes, it was holding together with only 5 bolts.  But I was on a mission to make everything under the hood perfect, and I just HAD to fix it (plus I already had the gasket). Well, I attacked the remnants of the bolt with my right angle drill and a screw extractor.  To my surprise, the broken-off bolt spun in about 1/4" as I was  drilling the pilot hole for the screw extractor.  It wasn't "rust welded" at all and came out very easily with the screw extractor.  This part of the job only took a few minutes and the only special tool required was the right angle drill.  Who knows, you too may be so lucky.  ||||reg||1
Z||000019||bHooper||02-24-2001||02:53 PM||||Roger,  the Permatex gasket material was not at fault.  in fact i am hoping that it saves me some trouble.  i also am using the saturn studs and flange nuts, Ed says they are better than oem.  <P>Mark, i just finished "sealing" everything up.  i am hoping that this gasket material will do the trick, for a while at least.<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000020||bHooper||02-25-2001||10:02 PM||||i just realized that i forgot to let everyone know that i cranked the car up today... boy does she sound nice!  even with only 5 studs holding the thing on.<P>and the only tick tick, is the click click of the injectors!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>of course, the reall test is the drive and tell.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's,<BR>Poly Bushings||||reg||1
